  "intro": "One-variable data are summarized by center (mean, median) and spread (range, standard deviation). The mean is the sum divided by the count; the median is the middle value in order. The mean is pulled by outliers while the median resists them. A larger standard deviation means more spread. On the SAT, you compare data sets, judge how outliers shift the mean versus the median, and read these from tables or graphs.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Find the mean of $4,8,6,10,12$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Add the values: $4+8+6+10+12=40$.",
          "Divide by the count $5$: $\\frac{40}{5}=8$."
        ],
        "answer": "$8$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Find the median of $3,7,8,5,12$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Order the values: $3,5,7,8,12$.",
          "The middle value is $7$."
        ],
        "answer": "$7$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Data set A is $4,5,6$; set B is $1,5,9$. Which has the larger standard deviation?",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Both have the same mean, $5$.",
          "Set B's values are farther from $5$ than set A's.",
          "So set B has the larger standard deviation."
        ],
        "answer": "Set B"
      }
    },
    {
